    {
      "factor": "Risk parameters and market conditions",
      "updatable": "Collateral factors, interest rate models, and oracle sources can be adjusted in response to volatility or a depegged asset.",
      "immutable": "Parameters are fixed at creation. You must launch a new market to change them, which fragments liquidity."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Security and bug response",
      "updatable": "Critical bugs can be paused or patched. This is a safety net, but it also introduces admin key risk and upgrade attack surface.",
      "immutable": "No patch is possible. Security depends entirely on audits, formal verification, and conservative design before launch."
    },
    {
      "factor": "User trust",
      "updatable": "Users must trust the governance or admin key holders not to change terms against them.",
      "immutable": "Users can verify the rules on-chain and know they cannot change, which is a strong selling point for lenders and borrowers who value credible neutrality."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Liquidity fragmentation",
      "updatable": "A single market can accumulate liquidity over time.",
      "immutable": "Each new parameter set creates a separate market, which can split liquidity across many pools."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Regulatory and integration needs",
      "updatable": "Compliance changes, such as adding or removing permitted assets, can be handled without redeploying.",
      "immutable": "Integrators and institutions may prefer fixed terms they can underwrite once."
    }
  ],
  "decision_framework": [
    "Choose updatable if the asset set is volatile or new, you expect frequent parameter tuning, you have a strong and accountable governance or multisig setup, and you can run timelocks on changes.",
    "Choose immutable if the asset set is well understood, the risk model is conservative and well tested, you want to maximize lender and borrower trust, and you can accept launching new markets to change terms.",
